{
 "clinical_assessment": "The patient has congenital long QT syndrome (Romano-Ward) and is currently on propranolol. Adding fluoxetine creates a dual hazard: fluoxetine prolongs the QT interval and propranolol is a beta-blocker that can mask bradycardia, compounding the risk of torsades de pointes.",
 "drug_rule": "Fluoxetine is a potent inhibitor of CYP2D6 and a moderate inhibitor of CYP1A2, CYP2C9, and CYP3A4. It also prolongs the QT interval via hERG channel blockade. Propranolol, a non-selective beta-blocker, can blunt compensatory tachycardia, reducing the heart's ability to maintain adequate perfusion under stress. The combination of two QT-prolonging agents in a patient with congenital channelopathy is contraindicated.",
