 "drug_rule": "Valacyclovir is a prodrug of acyclovir. In children, the recommended dose for herpes labialis is 20 mg/kg/day (maximum 800 mg/day) given twice daily. For a 10 kg child, this equates to 200 mg twice daily (400 mg/day). The prescribed dose of 2 g twice daily (4 g/day) is 10 times the maximum recommended pediatric dose, leading to a predictable and dangerous accumulation of acyclovir.",
 "patient_fit": "Although valacyclovir is generally safe in adults, the patient's young age and low body weight override any general safety assumptions. The prescribed dose far exceeds the weight-based pediatric limit, creating a high risk of acute kidney injury, neurotoxicity, and other dose-dependent adverse effects. This profile-specific miscalculation mandates the \"Unsafe\" verdict.",
